<h1>Conjugating Verbs in Spanish</h1>
<p>If you are a beggining Spanish speaker, you likely were being introduced to different phrases like 'Como estás'or 'Mucho gusto'. You may have realised that the same verbs end differeernetly depending on the context. Verbs in Spanish are conjugated with respect to what group of people they're refering to. It's like Subject verb agreement in English, verbs that refer to an action done by a single person end in 's' while those done by multiple people don't, however, the rules are more fufilling in Spanish.</p>
<p><strong>Spanish Pronouns:</strong></p>
<p>First we need to establish each set of pronoun in Spanish. In English we use pronouns like I, him, her, we, etc. Each subject pronoun has its own unique conjugation, so it is important to define them first.</p>
<p>Yo: first person('I' in English)</p>
<p>Tú: second person('you' in English)</p>
<p>Él, ella, usted: Third person(he, she, and you in English)</p>
<p>Nosotros: First person plural('we' in English)</p>
<p>Vosotros: Second person plural('You all' in English, Only ever used in Spain)</p>
<p>Ustedes: Second person plural('You all' in English, Used everywhere)</p>
<p><strong>Table For Conjugation</strong></p>
<p>There are 6 classification for Spanish subjects, so each will have their own unique ending for a particular verb</p>
<p>The base form of every verb in Spanish ends with either -ar, -er, or -ir. We call these the infinitives which is the same as adding 'to' right before a verb in English(the word 'comer' means 'to eat' in English). Whenever a verb is being performed by a subject, simply drop the last two letters in the infinitive form then add the appropriate ending(see table below)</p>
<img src="presente-de-indicativo-verbos-regulares.jpg">
<p>For example, If I have to say "We eat", in Spanish we would need to conjugate the verb "comer" by removing the er ending and replacing it with emos. The translated sentence becomes "Nosotros com<u>emos</u>."</p>
